###### Abstract

We have detected the optical counterpart to the nearby isolated neutron star RX J1605.3+3249 using observations from the Space Telescope Imaging Spectrometer aboard the _Hubble Space Telescope_. The counterpart, with m_{\\rm 50CCD}=26.84\\pm 0.07 mag and very blue colors, lies close to the _ROSAT_ HRI error circle and within the _Chandra_ error circle. The spectrum is consistent with a Rayleigh-Jeans tail whose emission is a factor of \\approx 14 above the extrapolation of the X-ray blackbody, and the source has an unabsorbed X-ray-to-optical flux ratio of \\log(f_{\\rm x}/f_{\\rm opt})=4.4, similar to that of other isolated neutron stars. This confirms the classification of RX J1605.3+3249 as a neutron star. pulsars: individual (RX J1605.3+3249)--stars: neutron--X-rays: stars +

